603.25187_stable_internal_beliefs_chunk_003.pdf\n./papers/pages/2603.25187_stable_internal_beliefs_chunk_004.pdf\n./papers/pages/2603.25187_stable_internal_beliefs_chunk_005.pdf\n./papers/pages/2603.25187_stable_internal_beliefs_chunk_006.pdf\n./papers/pages/2603.25187_stable_internal_beliefs_manifest.txt\n./papers/pages/2605.10794_keep_a_secret_chunk_001.pdf\n./papers/pages/2605.10794_keep_a_secret_chunk_002.pdf\n./papers/pages/2605.10794_keep_a_secret_chunk_003.pdf\n./papers/pages/2605.10794_keep_a_secret_chunk_004.pdf\n./papers/pages/2605.10794_keep_a_secret_chunk_005.pdf\n./papers/pages/2605.10794_keep_a_secret_chunk_006.pdf\n./papers/pages/2605.10794_keep_a_secret_manifest.txt\n./papers/pages/2608.19857_inadvertent_context_leakage_chunk_001.pdf\n./papers/pages/2608.19857_inadvertent_context_leakage_chunk_002.pdf\n./papers/pages/2608.19857_inadvertent_context_leakage_chunk_003.pdf\n./papers/pages/2608.19857_inadvertent_context_leakage_chunk_004.pdf\n./papers/pages/2608.19857_inadvertent_context_leakage_chunk_005.pdf\n./papers/pages/2608.19857_inadvertent_context_leakage_chunk_006.pdf\n./papers/pages/2608.19857_inadvertent_context_leakage_chunk_007.pdf\n./papers/pages/2608.19857_inadvertent_context_leakage_chunk_008.pdf\n./papers/pages/2608.19857_inadvertent_context_leakage_manifest.txt\n./planning.md\n./prompts/README.md\n./pyproject.toml\n./resources.md\n./results/evaluations/analysis_metadata.json\n./results/evaluations/behavior_summary.csv\n./results/evaluations/concept_directions.pt\n./results/evaluations/condition_contrasts.csv\n./results/evaluations/intervention_summary.csv\n./results/evaluations/intervention_summary_pairs.csv\n./results/evaluations/residual_projections.jsonl\n./results/evaluations/residual_projections_long.csv\n./results/evaluations/residual_summary.csv\n./results/evaluations/story_quality.csv\n./results/model_outputs/intervention_stories.jsonl\n./results/model_outputs/judgments_gemma.jsonl\n./results/model_outputs/judgments_interventions_gemma.jsonl\n./results/model_outputs/judgments_pilot.jsonl\n./results/model_outputs/judgments_pilot_gemma.jsonl\n./results/model_outputs/judgments_pilot_thinking_failed.jsonl\n./results/model_outputs/judgments_pilot_v1.jsonl\n./results/model_outputs/stories.jsonl\n./results/model_outputs/stories_pilot.jsonl\n./src/__pycache__/analyze.cpython-312.pyc\n./src/__pycache__/judge_interventions.cpython-312.pyc\n./src/__pycache__/run_behavioral.cpython-312.pyc\n./src/__pycache__/run_local.cpython-312.pyc\n./src/__pycache__/run_mechanism.cpython-312.pyc\n./src/analyze.py\n./src/judge_interventions.py\n./src/run_behavioral.py\n./src/run_local.py\n./src/run_mechanism.py\n./templates/paper_writing/lab_style_guide.md\n./templates/paper_writing/style_examples.md\n./uv.lock\nidea:\n  title: LLMs are bad at keeping obvious secrets\n  domain: artificial_intelligence\n  hypothesis: 'LLMs are bad at keeping obvious secrets. For instance, when LLMs write\n\n    fiction they foreshadow plot way too much because planning and prose\n\n    generation are entangled: the model can''t help but hint at what it knows is\n\n    coming. The model wants to say what it''s thinking, without being told it\n\n    has to. Is this still true when it can rely on a plan it''s conditioning on\n\n    that sets up the future so it doesn''t have to constantly get ready for\n\n    that future on its own?\n\n\n    Concretely: when a model writes a story while holding a secret it was told\n\n    not to reveal, does giving it an explicit plan or outline to follow reduce\n\n    how much of the secret leaks into the text? And what is the mechanism of\n\n    the leak: is the secret concept active in the model''s internal state\n\n    throughout generation, and does removing it remove the leakage?\n\n    '\n  background:\n    description: \"What is known. The idea's author has since published the behavioural\\\n      \\ core:\\nHoltzman and West, \\\"Can You Keep a Secret? Involuntary Information\\n\\\n      Leakage in Language Model Writing\\\" (arXiv 2605.10794, May 2026). A writer\\n\\\n      model gets a secret word plus an instruction not to mention or hint at\\nit,\\\n      \\ and writes a story of about 450 words; a guesser model then picks\\nwhich of\\\n      \\ two texts was written with the secret (two-alternative forced\\nchoice). They\\\n      \\ report 70-79% discrimination for several frontier models\\n(two were below\\\n      \\ chance), that an \\\"actively hide\\\" instruction pushes\\nleakage below chance,\\\n      \\ that short jokes do not leak, and that a decoy word\\nredirects the leakage.\\\n      \\ That work is behavioural and API-based, and the\\npaper itself says a more\\\n      \\ mechanistic study is needed. It used 15 secret\\nwords: umbrella, lighthouse,\\\n      \\ violin, cactus, telescope, justice,\\npatience, entropy, nostalgia, freedom,\\\n      \\ bracket, Tuesday, copper, margin,\\ninvoice. Related lines: semantic leakage\\\n      \\ (Gonen et al. 2025); Taboo\\nmodel organisms where a secret word is fine-tuned\\\n      \\ in and recovered with\\nlogit lens and SAEs (Cywinski et al.), which does not\\\n      \\ address\\ninvoluntary leakage into unrelated text; secrecy without private\\\n      \\ state\\n(Baldelli et al.); ironic negation (Mann et al.); and evidence that\\n\\\n      models plan future tokens in hidden states (2502.06258, 2404.00859, and\\nAnthropic's\\\n      \\ planning-in-poems result).\\n\\nGap. Two things appear untouched. (1) The idea's\\\n      \\ actual question: does\\nconditioning on an explicit plan or outline reduce\\\n      \\ leakage, and does it\\nhold for plot-level secrets (a twist the model knows\\\n      \\ is coming) rather\\nthan only single words? (2) Mechanism: is the secret represented\\\n      \\ in the\\nresidual stream throughout story generation, does its strength predict\\n\\\n      leakage, and does ablating it remove leakage? A run that only replicates\\nthe\\\n      \\ published secret-word effect adds little.\\n\\nScope and pitfalls.\\n- Model-size\\\n      \\ threshold (the main risk). The prior paper reports NO\\n  leakage for Llama\\\n      \\ 3.2-3B and Llama 3.1-8B, marginal leakage for Gemma 3\\n  4B (59%), and strong\\\n      \\ leakage (83-84%) for Gemma 3 12B/27B and Llama 3.3\\n  70B. A writer model\\\n      \\ of about 8-9B or smaller is therefore expected to\\n  show a floor effect with\\\n      \\ nothing to explain. Gemma 3 12B is the\\n  smallest model reported to leak\\\n      \\ strongly.\\n- Models: gated repos (google/gemma-*, meta-llama/*) need the HF_TOKEN\\n\\\n      \\  environment variable to have been granted access; if a download is\\n  refused,\\\n      \\ unsloth/gemma-3-12b-it works without it. SAEs for this model\\n  are in google/gemma-scope-2-12b-it\\\n      \\ (ungated; newer and less supported\\n  tooling). An OpenRouter API key is available\\\n      \\ as OPENROUTER_KEY (for\\n  example for the guesser model).\\n- Confound in any\\\n      \\ plan condition: an outline both adds context (which\\n  may just dilute the\\\n      \\ secret's salience) and fixes content decisions. A\\n  comparison is only informative\\\n      \\ if it can tell these apart, for example\\n  against length-matched irrelevant\\\n      \\ context.\\n- The known effects give yardsticks: the plain \\\"secret plus do\\\n      \\ not\\n  reveal\\\" condition is a positive anchor, and the decoy word is a known\\n\\\n      \\  mitigation to compare effect sizes against.\\n- Measurement: guesser judgments\\\n      \\ are noisy and position-biased, so both\\n  presentation orders and enough trials\\\n      \\ are needed to separate, say, 80%\\n  from 65%. For plot-level secrets, candidate\\\n      \\ twists may be guessable\\n  from the premise alone, so a no-secret control\\\n      \\ is needed. For\\n  ablations, compare against random and other-concept directions\\\n      \\ and\\n  check that story quality is not simply degraded.\\n\"\n    papers:\n    - url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.10794\n      description: Holtzman and West, Can You Keep a Secret? Involuntary Information\n        Leakage in Language Model Writing (May 2026). The behavioural core of this\n        idea, by its author; does not test a plan/outline condition, plot-level secrets,\n        or mechanism.\n    - url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.14352\n      description: Cywinski et al., Towards eliciting latent knowledge from LLMs with\n        mechanistic interpretability. Taboo model organisms (fine-tuned Gemma-2-9b);\n        logit lens and SAEs recover the secret word.\n    - url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01070\n      description: Eliciting Secret Knowledge from Language Models. Taboo-style secret\n        elicitation; the secret is fine-tuned in and the model is meant to hint.\n    - url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.06973\n      description: 'Baldelli et al., LLMs Can''t Play Hangman: On the Necessity of\n        a Private Working Memory for Language Agents. Impossibility result on secrecy\n        plus consistency without private state.'\n    - url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25187\n      description: Probing the Lack of Stable Internal Beliefs in LLMs (20-questions\n        implicit consistency).\n    - url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.12381\n      description: 'Mann et al., Don''t think of the white bear: ironic negation in\n        transformer models.'\n    - url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.17884\n      description: Mireshghallah et al., Can LLMs Keep a Secret? Contextual-integrity\n        view of secret keeping.\n    - url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06258\n      description: Emergent Response Planning in LLMs. Planning information in hidden\n        states.\n    - url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.00859\n      description: Do Language Models Plan Ahead for Future Tokens?\n    - url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.17119\n      description: Suspense generation with outlines; evaluates suspense, not leakage.\n  metadata:\n    author: Ari Holtzman\n    idea_id: llms_are_bad_at_keeping_obviou_20261003_015558_556756e7\n    created_at: '2026-10-03T01:55:58.669267'\n    status: submitted\n    github_repo_name: llms-are-bad-at-keeping-obvious-secrets-72ad-codex-gpt-5-6-sol\n    github_repo_url: https://github.com/Hypogenic-AI/llms-are-bad-at-keeping-obvious-secrets-72ad-codex-gpt-5-6-sol\n    github_repo_private: false\ntotal 232\ndrwxrwsrwx  3 neurico 27500      7 Oct  3 04:26 .\ndrwxrwsrwx 22 neurico 27500     32 Oct  3 04:26 ..\ndrwxrwsrwx  2 neurico 27500      5 Oct  3 04:26 commands\n-rw-rw-rw-  1 neurico 27500 171133 Oct  3 04:26 neurips_2025.pdf\n-rw-rw-rw-  1 neurico 27500  13023 Oct  3 04:26 neurips_2025.sty\n-rw-rw-rw-  1 neurico 27500  44551 Oct  3 04:26 neurips_2025.tex\n-rw-rw-rw-  1 neurico 27500    135 Oct  3 04:26 style_config.yaml\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"mkdir -p paper_draft/sections paper_draft/figures paper_draft/tables paper_draft/appendix paper && sed -n '1,320p' REPORT.md && sed -n '1,260p' papers/deep_read_notes.md && column -s, -t results/evaluations/behavior_summary.csv && column -s, -t results/evaluations/condition_contrasts.csv && column -s, -t results/evaluations/intervention_summary.csv && column -s, -t results/evaluations/residual_summary.csv\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"mkdir -p paper_draft/sections paper_draft/figures paper_draft/tables paper_draft/appendix paper && sed -n '1,320p' REPORT.md && sed -n '1,260p' papers/deep_read_notes.md && column -s, -t results/evaluations/behavior_summary.csv && column -s, -t results/evaluations/condition_contrasts.csv && column -s, -t results/evaluations/intervention_summary.csv && column -s, -t results/evaluations/residual_summary.csv\"","aggregated_output":"# LLMs Are Bad at Keeping Obvious Secrets\n\n## 1. Executive Summary\n\nWe tested whether an explicit outline reduces involuntary secret leakage and whether secret-aligned residual activity explains it. On local Gemma 3 12B, an unconstrained open-story anchor replicated leakage: the secret-aware story received a **+16.3 point** higher order-balanced influence score than its no-secret control (15 pairs, 95% bootstrap CI **+4.7 to +29.1**, permutation **p=.020**). However, a neutral story brief removed the detectable signal before an outline was added (no-outline mean −0.15, CI −2.87 to +2.40). The outline contrast was inconclusive: outline minus no outline was −2.50 (30 pairs, CI −6.83 to +1.85, p=.280), and outline minus length-matched irrelevant context was −2.08 (CI −7.12 to +2.95, p=.418).\n\nThe defensible conclusion is that **content constraints, not demonstrated planning offload, eliminated detectable word leakage here**. Plot-twist trials were underpowered and null. Carrier-prompt directions showed tiny, inconsistent secret/no-secret projection gaps. Projection removal strongly inverted judgments, but catastrophically shortened stories (mean 102 versus 488 words for random removal), so it does not establish selective causality.\n\n## 2. Research Question & Motivation\n\nHoltzman and West (2026) showed that frontier models leak forbidden words into unrelated stories, but did not test plans, plot secrets, or internals. We ask whether an external outline offloads future decisions and whether leakage is mediated by a persistent secret representation. A length-matched irrelevant-context condition separates planning from context dilution.\n\n## 3. Experimental Setup\n\n### Models, compute, and access\n\n- Writer and primary judge: `google/gemma-3-12b-it`, local Unsloth mirror revision `9478e665381f42974aa06177b019352fb6291876`, BF16.\n- Failed cross-family sensitivity judge: `Qwen/Qwen3-14B`, revision `40c069824f4251a91eefaf281ebe4c544efd3e18`.\n- NVIDIA RTX A6000 (49,140 MiB); peak generation residency about 36 GB.\n- Python 3.12.8, PyTorch 2.7.1, Transformers 4.53.3; full versions in `pyproject.toml`/`uv.lock`.\n- OpenRouter was catalog-verified on 2026-10-03 but returned a daily-limit 403; the direct OpenAI key returned 401. All reported outputs are from real local models, never simulations.\n\n### Data and protocol\n\nAll 15 published secrets were crossed with two neutral briefs and four contexts: no outline, secret-blind outline, length-matched irrelevant context, and the paper's fixed-offset decoy. Each secret story had a matched no-secret story. A separate open anchor used no brief. Six plot twists used twist-aware versus premise-only writers under no outline, outline, and irrelevant context.\n\nThere were **306 confirmatory stories** plus 80 archived extra-brief exploratory stories. There were zero literal secret mentions and zero empty outputs. Word count was 369–608 (mean 534), above target because the token minimum delayed EOS. Exact prompts and seeds are in `src/run_behavioral.py`. Sampling used temperature .8/top-p .95; judging was greedy.\n\nEvery pair was judged in both orders with per-text 0–100 scores. The primary endpoint maps both orders to `secret-present score − control score` and averages them, algebraically cancelling position bias. We report pair-level 10,000-sample bootstrap intervals and two-sided sign-permutation tests. The intended mixed model was not used because only two confirmatory briefs poorly identify its random effects.\n\n## 4. Results\n\n| Task | Condition | Pairs | Mean score difference | 95% CI | Sign accuracy | p |\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| Word | Open anchor | 15 | **+16.33** | **[+4.73, +29.10]** | 73.3% | .020 |\n| Word | No outline | 30 | −0.15 | [−2.87, +2.40] | 51.7% | .932 |\n| Word | Outline | 30 | −2.65 | [−6.28, +0.72] | 46.7% | .173 |\n| Word | Irrelevant context | 30 | −0.57 | [−4.23, +3.37] | 46.7% | .791 |\n| Word | Decoy | 30 | **−3.60** | **[−6.70, −0.62]** | 28.3% | .025 |\n| Plot | No outline | 6 | +2.33 | [−7.58, +13.33] | 41.7% | .811 |\n| Plot | Outline | 6 | 0.00 | [−10.83, +11.25] | 50.0% | 1.000 |\n| Plot | Irrelevant | 6 | +8.67 | [−2.50, +18.17] | 75.0% | .192 |\n\n![Behavioral score differences](figures/behavior_delta.png)\n\nThe anchor shows the checkpoint and endpoint can recover the known effect. The brief then causes a floor. Outline minus no-outline was −2.50 [−6.83, +1.85], p=.280; outline minus irrelevant was −2.08 [−7.12, +2.95], p=.418. Decoy inversion qualitatively reproduces the published yardstick.\n\nJudge reliability was limited: Gemma order consistency was 10–40% for word conditions. Qwen achieved only 4/22 consistent pilot pairs and was excluded. Order-mapped scores reduce position bias, but instability remains the dominant measurement limitation.\n\n### Residual stream\n\nDirections were fitted from four independent carrier pairs per secret, never evaluation prose or labels. We projected layers 12/24/36/47 at 10%/50%/90% of prose. Gaps were tiny and inconsistent. The largest mean secret-control gap was +.0132 at layer 47/late without an outline; the outlined gap there was −.00144.\n\n![Residual trajectory](figures/residual_trajectory.png)\n\nThis does not support persistent carrier-defined activation, but cannot rule out distributed or context-dependent secret representations.\n\n### Causal intervention\n\nFor six secrets, we projected out the carrier direction at layers 24 and 36; seeded random directions were controls. Secret removal produced mean judged delta −77.5, 0/6 positive signs, and 6/6 order-consistent inversions, versus −10.8 and 1/6 positives for random removal. But secret-removal stories averaged **102 words** (range 9–354), versus **488** (466–502) for random removal. The intervention caused severe generic damage, fails the utility criterion, and cannot demonstrate selective causal removal.\n\nRaw outputs are in `results/model_outputs/`, processed results in `results/evaluations/`, and figures in `figures/`.\n\n## 5. Discussion\n\nThe positive open anchor and null briefed condition indicate that unconstrained content selection is a major leakage carrier. A brief already fixes setting, characters, and conflict—the same choices an outline further specifies. This floor is informative but prevents a clean test of planning offload once leakage is present. The outline did not outperform irrelevant context; this rejects a strong benefit claim in this setup, but its interval allows modest benefit or harm.\n\nPlot results are inconclusive: six heterogeneous twists provide little power. The residual/intervention results caution against equating a templated mean-difference direction with the causal representation.\n\n## 6. Limitations\n\n- One writer and one usable primary judge; cross-family judging failed reliability checks.\n- 30 word pairs and only six plot pairs per condition.\n- Severe judge order instability, reduced but not eliminated by score mapping.\n- Brief-induced floor; output lengths above target.\n- Carrier directions were linear/template-dependent; intervention severely degraded utility.\n- API failure prevented frontier-judge replication and a human audit.\n- Intervals do not generalize over models.\n\nEvidence quality is **moderate** for the anchor and brief-induced floor, **low** for outline equivalence, and **very low** for plot and causal claims.\n\n## 7. Conclusions & Next Steps\n\nGemma 3 12B leaked secret words when story content was unconstrained, but leakage disappeared when a neutral brief fixed content. Adding an outline did not improve on either the brief or irrelevant context. This run therefore identifies content-choice freedom as a critical moderator rather than demonstrating planning offload.\n\nFollow-up should screen broad premises that retain 65–80% anchor discrimination, use 100+ pairs per condition with two calibrated judges and blinded humans, scale premise-calibrated twists, and learn causal subspaces with LEACE/INLP on disjoint secrets while rejecting any intervention that materially alters length or coherence.\n\n## References\n\nHoltzman & West (2026), arXiv:2605.10794; Gonen et al. (2025), arXiv:2408.06518; Cywiński et al. (2025), arXiv:2505.14352/2510.01070; Dong et al. (2025), arXiv:2502.06258; Wu et al. (2024), arXiv:2404.00859; Mann et al. (2025), arXiv:2511.12381; Xie & Riedl (2024), arXiv:2402.17119; Fairoze et al. (2026), arXiv:2608.19857.\n# Deep-reading notes\n\nAll user-specified papers were split into three-page chunks. Notes below record the sequential full-text pass; chunk manifests are in `papers/pages/`.\n\n## 2605.10794 — Can You Keep a Secret?\n\n- Chunks 1–2 (pp. 1–6): Writer–guesser design with 15 curated secrets, four text tasks, five prompt conditions, free-response guessing and order-balanced 2AFC. Literal leakage was absent, but thematic leakage reached 79.3%. Active hiding often inverted the signal rather than eliminating it.\n- Chunks 3–4 (pp. 7–12): Cross-model transfer supports a text-level signal; within-family scaling jumps around 12B+; short stock jokes are a negative-control task, while long-form stories/jokes/essays leak. User-prompt placement changes some model behavior. A decoy concept redirects part, not all, of the signal. Limitations include single-word toy secrets, API model drift, model-as-judge dependence, and limited mechanistic evidence.\n- Chunks 5–6 (pp. 13–18): Exact prompts, binomial tests with FDR/Bonferroni corrections, qualitative examples, alternate COCA/model-chosen word sets, position-bias controls, and deterministic decoy pairings. Recommended reproduction controls: both presentation orders, no-secret baselines, literal-secret filtering, fixed pairings/seeds, and separate free-response from discrimination outcomes.\n\nActionable implication: the natural behavioral extension is a preregistered factorial test of `no plan / secret-free plan / secret-aware plan`, with plan content held fixed where possible. The key mechanistic prediction is that a plan should reduce leakage only if it reduces secret decodability in generation-time residual states.\n\n## 2505.14352 — Towards eliciting latent knowledge from LLMs with mechanistic interpretability\n\n- Chunks 1–2 (pp. 1–6): Twenty Gemma-2-9B QLoRA “Taboo” models each internalize a one-token secret absent from their prompts/training strings, using 3–5-turn synthetic hint dialogues. Black-box baselines include naive/adversarial prompting, prefill token forcing, and an external guesser. White-box methods average layer-32 logit-lens probabilities or rank Gemma Scope SAE features over response positions.\n- Chunk 2 results: another-model guessing gives 95% Pass@10; post-game token forcing 70%; logit lens top-5 75%; SAE top-5 55%. Naive prompting is 0%. The SAE evaluation is not fully discovery-free: secret-to-latent mappings are manually identified, while logit lens is advantaged by single-token targets.\n- Chunks 3–7 (pp. 7–19): Complete synthetic-data prompts, validation rules, QLoRA hyperparameters (rank 8, 10 epochs, batch 8, LR 2e-4, A100), rollout examples, attack prompts, token-forcing protocol, manually curated SAE mappings, and per-token activation plots. Limitations: simple organism, only 20 common one-token secrets, model outputs deliberately describe the secret, and no causal intervention.\n\nActionable implication: use layerwise linear probes as the primary low-cost internal-state measure; reserve SAE/logit-lens ranking as secondary analyses. Add activation steering/erasure with matched random-direction controls to establish causal rather than merely decodable secret representations.\n\n## 2510.01070 — Eliciting Secret Knowledge from Language Models\n\n- Chunks 1–4 (pp. 1–12): Three model-organism families: Gemma-2-9B Taboo words, Llama-3.3-70B Base64 secret side constraints, and Gemma-2-9B inferred user gender. Training mixes 10× unrelated Alpaca turns to reduce artificial fine-tuning traces. Auditing compares adversarial prompting, assistant prefills, user-persona sampling, many-shot jailbreaks, activation fuzzing, logit lens, activation–embedding similarity, and SAE descriptions/tokens.\n- Core results: on standard prompts, white-box information improves auditing in most settings (e.g. Taboo I/O 42.1% versus activation tokens 85.1%; user-gender I/O 51.8% versus SAE descriptions 85.4%). On direct prompts, assistant prefill/user-persona attacks exceed 95% for side-constraint and gender models, but remain weak for Taboo. Fuzzing/high temperature/many-shot attacks are mostly ineffective. Untuned-base controls generally remove the white-box gains.\n- Chunks 5–8 (pp. 13–24): LoRA and dataset details, internalization evaluations, held-out standard/direct prompt sets, and detailed score tables. The benchmark uses smaller auditors and GPT-4/4.1 judges; SSC evaluation is semantic similarity over five guesses rather than exact recovery.\n- Chunks 9–15 (pp. 25–45): Layer/token-position choices are model-specific and tuned on validation: assistant control tokens for Taboo, Base64 spans for SSC, first-person pronouns for gender. Prefill results are highly prefix-sensitive. A multilingual prefill bypass reveals that Taboo suppresses an English token more than the underlying concept. Multi-stage auditor pipelines filter large white-box readouts, creating extra modeling choices.\n- Chunks 16–19 (pp. 46–55): Raw readouts show decoded constraint words near completion of their Base64 spans and strongest secret signals at assistant control tokens. SAE coverage and description quality limit recovery; activation–embedding tokens can be noisy or surface both genders. The paper explicitly notes simple organisms, single SFT acquisition, easy multi-turn behavioral inference, and lack of organically learned secrets.\n\nActionable implication: probe the secret across prompt, outline, and generation positions; include base-model/random-direction controls; and test causal removal at the same positions. Do not infer causality from probe accuracy alone, and avoid selecting intervention layers on the final test set.\n\n## 2601.06973 — LLMs Can't Play Hangman\n\n- Chunks 1–2 (pp. 1–6): Defines Private State Interactive Tasks and proves that a public-only chat policy cannot guarantee both public underdetermination and consistency with a fixed privately generated secret. The theorem concerns persistence across turns, not one-shot thematic leakage.\n- Chunks 3–4 (pp. 7–12): Forked self-consistency testing on Hangman and a DDXPlus diagnosis simulator. Across 50 episodes per condition, vanilla and retrieval-memory agents score poorly; explicit private-state workflows often reach 90–100%, while private chain-of-thought is a costly upper bound. Workflow updates outperform autonomous tool choice; explicit memory remains mostly under 100 tokens versus roughly 10× larger private-CoT context.\n- Chunks 5–8 (pp. 13–24): Related work, structured working-memory design, qualitative memory snapshots, model/secret distributions, and robustness discussion. Retrieval systems preserve public utterances but omit the self-generated secret; overwrite/patch memory explicitly retains it.\n- Chunks 9–12 (pp. 25–36): Fork-point ablations preserve method ordering; complete task/agent prompts and memory tool schemas are provided. Key limitations for this project: the theorem does not imply that an external outline reduces one-turn leakage, and explicit private memory may actually preserve secret activation rather than suppress it.\n\nActionable implication: distinguish `external plan` (public conditioning text) from `private persistent state`. The present hypothesis is about whether an outline offloads anticipatory generation, not whether a memory channel can remember a secret across turns.\n\n## 2603.25187 — Probing the Lack of Stable Internal Beliefs in LLMs\n\n- Chunks 1–2 (pp. 1–6): In number/entity 20-questions games, branch probes ask for the selected target index after every turn. Reported once-drift is typically near 100%, while per-turn drift ranges roughly 11–100%; reasoning can worsen latent drift even while improving visible consistency. Qwen-2.5-14B fine-tuning with a KL-to-initial-belief loss reduces drift more than CE alone.\n- Chunks 3–6 (pp. 7–17): Appendices specify prompts, supplementary drift/KL metrics, training/evaluation details, broader impacts, and checklist. Main caveat: the “internal belief” is inferred through an explicit behavioral probe distribution, not a trained activation probe, and the branch question can measure answer instability rather than a causally persistent hidden state.\n\nActionable implication: for this project, measure the secret with both behavioral counterfactual probes and activation probes. Treat either alone as evidence of accessibility, not proof of a stable belief.\n\n## 2511.12381 — Don't Think of the White Bear\n\n- Chunks 1–2 (pp. 1–6): ReboundBench has about 5,000 templated English prompts with single-token forbidden nouns, three distractor types, and lengths to 1,024 tokens. It evaluates forbidden-token log probability, not generated-text leakage. Semantic distractors generally produce stronger/more persistent rebound; repetition usually supports suppression. Head ablations on Llama-3-8B-Instruct attribute much of the effect to a sparse set of middle-layer heads.\n- Chunks 3–4 (pp. 7–12): Dataset construction, mixed/robust regression tables, model-specific peak/AUC/L50 results, limitations, and attention-head details. Important reservations: high-load values define the baseline used to call lower-load effects “rebound”; actual narrative generation and multi-token concepts are untested; head-ablation causality is narrow and only detailed for one model.\n\nActionable implication: include positive/decoy framing and secret-omission controls, because a literal “do not mention X” condition may create the very activation being measured. Use story-level discrimination as the primary outcome and next-token rebound only as a supporting mechanism measure.\n\n## 2310.17884 — ConfAIde\n\n- Chunks 1–3 (pp. 1–9): Four tiers progress from sensitivity ratings to contextual vignettes, theory-of-mind secret sharing, and meeting summaries/action items. Tier 3 uses 270 synthetic scenarios; Tier 4 uses 20 transcripts. GPT-4 leaks in 22% of Tier-3 generations and inappropriate information flows occur in 39% of Tier-4 summaries; ChatGPT rates are substantially higher. Explicit privacy prompts help but do not solve leakage.\n- Main mitigation result: a simple chain-of-thought prompt slightly worsens leakage and utility. Manual error analysis finds many chains acknowledge privacy but fail to operationalize it, or falsely assume the recipient already knows the secret.\n- Chunks 4–8 (pp. 10–24): References, exact factor sets/prompts, annotation details, metric tables, and context breakdowns. Inter-annotator Krippendorff alpha is low (0.07–0.34), emphasizing norm ambiguity. Leakage is direct contextual disclosure, distinct from the involuntary thematic channel in the target hypothesis.\n\nActionable implication: retain a clear utility–privacy tradeoff metric. An outline that suppresses all secret-adjacent content could lower leakage while degrading narrative quality or coherence, so both must be measured.\n\n## 2502.06258 — Emergent Response Planning in LLMs\n\n- Chunks 1–2 (pp. 1–6): One-hidden-layer MLP probes predict six future response attributes from prompt-end residual states: length, reasoning steps, animal character, final MCQ answer, answer correctness, and factual consistency. Results generalize across paired datasets and exceed class/random baselines for eight 7–8B base/instruction models.\n- Chunks 3–4 (pp. 7–12): Planning decodability scales within model families, follows a high–low–high trajectory over generation, and substantially exceeds models’ verbalized self-predictions. Authors explicitly acknowledge that probes establish correlation, not causal influence, and greedy decoding removes sampling uncertainty.\n- Chunks 5–7 (pp. 13–20): Exact models/datasets, prompts, filtering/augmentation, representation collection, and full regression plots. The character task is especially relevant but only predicts one of the four most frequent animals after balancing/filtering; prompt features may still contain predictive correlations.\n\nActionable implication: model the proposed mechanism as mediation: `outline condition → lower secret decodability during prose generation → lower story leakage`. Pre-register prompt-end and tokenwise probe locations, and add causal erasure because response-planning decodability alone is insufficient.\n\n## 2404.00859 — Do Language Models Plan Ahead for Future Tokens?\n\n- Chunks 1–3 (pp. 1–9): Separates deliberate “pre-caching” from “breadcrumbs” that are useful now and later. Myopic training removes off-diagonal future-loss gradients. Synthetic tasks show strong necessary pre-caching; GPT-2 natural-language loss shows a small myopia gap (3.40 versus 3.28) and near-zero local myopia bonus, favoring breadcrumbs at small scale.\n- Chunks 4–5 (pp. 10–15): Pythia 14M–2.8B results suggest the vanilla–myopic gap grows with scale. Discussion, references, and formal decomposition clarify that future-token decodability is not by itself deliberate planning.\n- Chunks 6–8 (pp. 16–24): Convergence assumptions/proofs, architecture/training details, multiplication/filler-token experiments, and gradient analysis. Natural-language experiments use short 64-token contexts and models at most 2.8B, limiting direct transfer to frontier story generation.\n\nActionable implication: the outline hypothesis has two competing mechanisms: it may reduce deliberate pre-caching, or it may leave semantic breadcrumbs unchanged. A successful experiment must measure both behavior and the temporal profile of secret representations.\n\n## 2402.17119 — Suspenseful Story Generation by Iteratively Refining a Plot\n\n- Chunks 1–2 (pp. 1–6): The method first generates a background, then iteratively constructs action–failure pairs that make the protagonist's goal progressively less likely, and finally elaborates details that plant clues while delaying their explanation. This explicit separation of plot construction from prose realization is the closest methodological precedent for testing whether an external plan reduces anticipatory leakage.\n- Chunks 3–4 (pp. 7–12): Human pairwise evaluation strongly favors the full pipeline over direct ChatGPT generation for suspense (84.9%), novelty (76.6%), and enjoyment (67.9%). Logical consistency is 49.2% versus 27.8%, with naturalness mostly tied. It also beats Llama-2 and Re3 baselines on suspense. Ablations show that both iterative outlining and detail elaboration contribute.\n- Chunks 5–6 (pp. 13–17): Outline diagnostics find 96.5% action relevance, 89.8% plausible failures, but only 55.1% of consecutive steps clearly decrease goal likelihood. Planted clues improve suspense judgments (57.9% versus 10.9%); information-timing gains are not significant. Appendices provide generation and evaluation prompts, examples, and annotator instructions.\n\nActionable implication: reuse the plan/prose separation, but independently control whether the outline is secret-blind or secret-aware. Otherwise an apparent benefit of planning could merely reflect changing how often the model is exposed to the secret.\n\n## 2408.06518 — Does Liking Yellow Imply Driving a School Bus?\n\n- Chunks 1–2 (pp. 1–6): Defines semantic leakage as an unrelated prompt concept exerting excess semantic influence on a completion. The 109 paired test/control prompts cover colors, food, animals, names, music, occupations, and idioms. Leak-Rate is the fraction for which a test completion is closer to the concept than its matched control under BERTScore, Sentence-BERT, or OpenAI embeddings. Across 13 GPT/Llama variants and four temperatures, all models exceed the 50% null; instruction-tuned variants generally leak more.\n- Chunks 3–5 (pp. 7–14): Human leakage judgments reach 71.8% on a validation subset (inter-annotator Kendall tau 0.68), while automatic metrics range 70.5–84.9%. Leakage persists in Hebrew, Chinese, cross-lingual prompts, stories, and recipes. A 23-name story test yields 60.9–78.7% depending on embedding metric. The test set is small and handcrafted, automatic scores can confound prompt repetition, and the work tests association rather than secrecy or causal mechanisms.\n\nActionable implication: use matched secret/no-secret outputs and blinded 2AFC judgments as the primary leakage measure; embedding similarity is a useful secondary metric but should not stand alone.\n\n## 2608.19857 — Inadvertent Context Leakage in Language Models\n\n- Chunks 1–3 (pp. 1–9): Formalizes black-box predicate inference from benign outputs conditioned on protected in-context data. Per-model decoders trained on self-authored contexts reconstruct two-digit secrets perfectly for some frontier models and four-digit secrets at up to 82% exact match; randomized-label controls remain at chance. A semantic-memory experiment improves advantage over a model-agnostic linguistic judge (0.319 versus 0.058). An active prompt-injection attack additionally extracts SSNs, but that is a distinct, stronger threat model.\n- Chunks 4–8 (pp. 10–24): Proposes suppression-induced probability redistribution as the passive mechanism. Suppression and leakage correlate across eight models (Spearman rho 0.95), though the small sample and tier separation drive the result. More emphatic confidentiality instructions can increase leakage; co-present distractors stay at chance while the protected value is recoverable. Appendices detail LoRA decoders, shuffled-label controls, instruction transfer, digit-stripped length/style/embedding channels, costs, and limitations. The study is black-box, primarily numeric, and does not test free-text story plans or representation-level interventions.\n\nActionable implication: include a secret-free control and an active-hiding condition, test classifiers on held-out secrets rather than only held-out generations, and distinguish passive prose leakage from an optimized adversarial exfiltration channel.\n/bin/bash: line 1: column: command not found\n","exit_code":127,"status":"failed"}}
